## Iterate behind the gate, never in front of it

Refinement works when something outside you can say whether the last
attempt was better. That is what the analyzer is. With it, iterating
converges. Without it, iterating measurably makes things WORSE — models
asked to revise their own work with no external signal degrade round
over round, and when they change an answer they are more likely to break
a correct one than to fix a wrong one.

So every round must be anchored to a measurement. "I think that reads
better now" is not a round, it is drift.

## The budget

Most of the available improvement lands in the FIRST correction. The
second is worth less, the third is usually the last one worth taking,
and quality past that is not monotonic — it goes down as often as up.

- Fix the specific measured failure. Do not rewrite what was passing.
- Three honest attempts at the same failure is the limit. Then stop and
  tell the user what you tried and what it measured each time.
- Escalating early is not giving up. It is the correct move when the
  remaining question is one only they can answer — anything about
  proportion, style, or what the thing is supposed to be.

## Carry the lesson, not just the fix

When a failure turns out to have a cause you did not expect, record
three things in your answer: what the error was, what actually caused
it, and the check that would have caught it. That last one is the
valuable part and the one most often skipped.

A fix without its guard is a fix you will need again.
